    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1874</span><span class="rf-text">Winston Churchill is born at Blenheim Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text">He is elected Conservative MP for Oldham, aged 25, after his escape from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> prisoner-of-war camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">The naval attack on the Dardanelles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> fail, and he is removed from the Admiralty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">As Chancellor of the Exchequer he returns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a> to the gold standard</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">He becomes prime minister and resolves to fight on after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> surrenders; weeks later the Conservatives lose the general election to Labour</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1951</span><span class="rf-text">He returns as prime minister, serving until his resignation in 1955</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">Churchill dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and is given a state funeral</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Winston Leonard Spencer Churchill was born on 30 November 1874 at Blenheim Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, his family's ancestral home. Through his father, the Conservative MP Lord Randolph Churchill, he belonged to the aristocracy as a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</a>; his mother, Jennie, was a daughter of the American businessman Leonard Jerome. For much of the 1880s his parents were effectively estranged, and he and his brother Jack were cared for by their nanny, Elizabeth Everest, whom he later called his "dearest and most intimate friend".</p><p>He was not academic and his behaviour was poor at his first boarding school, but he improved at Brunswick School in Hove and in 1888 entered Harrow, where his father had him prepare for the army. After two failed attempts he entered the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, as a cavalry cadet in 1893. His father died in January 1895.</p><p>Commissioned into the 4th Queen's Own Hussars in February 1895, he used his mother's influence to see action. He observed the Cuban War of Independence, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he read widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, Gibbon, Darwin and Macaulay, and joined the Malakand Field Force on the North West Frontier, the subject of his first book. In September 1898 he rode in one of the British Army's last cavalry charges, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and wrote The River War. He left the army, critical of Kitchener's unmerciful treatment of enemy wounded.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers Childhood and schooling: 1874–1895, Cuba, India, and Sudan: 1895–1899, Politics and South Africa: 1899–1901, Conservative MP: 1901–1904:</p><ul><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Geoffrey Best, Churchill: A Study in Greatness (Hambledon and Continuum, 2001)</li><li>Robert Rhodes James, Churchill: A Study in Failure 1900–1939 (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1970)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>He lost the Oldham by-election of June 1899 and went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> as a journalist for the Morning Post. Captured when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> artillery derailed his train, he escaped from a prison camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> in December and reached Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, an escape that attracted much publicity. In October 1900 he was elected Conservative MP for Oldham, aged 25.</p><p>A free trader, he opposed the Conservatives' turn to protectionism, and in May 1904 he attacked the Aliens Bill, designed to curb Jewish immigration, for its "racial prejudice against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>". On 31 May 1904 he crossed the floor to the Liberals, a defection that may have been influenced by ambition. Under Asquith he became President of the Board of Trade in 1908, the youngest Cabinet member since 1866, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> championed social reform: trade boards that established the principle of a minimum wage, labour exchanges and the National Insurance Act 1911, which he helped draft. In September 1908 he married Clementine Hozier, his wife until his death. As Home Secretary from 1910 he reformed the prisons and commuted 21 of 43 death sentences.</p><p>In October 1911 Asquith made him First Lord of the Admiralty. He built up the navy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, encouraged naval aviation, coined the word "seaplane" and in 1914 secured for the government a 51% share in the profits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Persian Oil Company</a> to supply the fleet.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers Conservative MP: 1901–1904, Liberal MP: 1904–1908, Election result and cabinet appointments:</p><ul><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Robert Rhodes James, Churchill: A Study in Failure 1900–1939 (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1970)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, Churchill became prime minister on 10 May 1940, the day German forces attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. He formed a war cabinet with Labour, made himself Minister of Defence and, at the end of May, overcame Lord Halifax's proposal to explore a peace settlement through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>. His speeches, from "blood, toil, tears and sweat" to "we shall fight on the beaches" and "their finest hour", hardened opinion against a peaceful resolution; Roy Jenkins called them "an inspiration for the nation". Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a> and the Blitz he ordered the Special Operations Executive and the Commandos into being.</p><p>His good relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> helped secure supplies and, from 1941, Lend-Lease; in August 1941 they issued the Atlantic Charter. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> he agreed with Roosevelt that Germany should be defeated first. He met Stalin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1942 and in the "Big Three" conferences at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1943 and Yalta in 1945, and argued for invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> in February 1942, which he called "the worst disaster and largest capitulation in British military history", the tide turned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>. Allied forces landed in Normandy on 6 June 1944, and on 8 May 1945 he announced Germany's surrender.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers President of the Board of Trade: 1908–1910, Home Secretary: 1910–1911, First Lord of the Admiralty, Minister of Munitions: 1917–1919:</p><ul><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Robert Rhodes James, Churchill: A Study in Failure 1900–1939 (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1970)</li><li>Robert Blake (ed.), Churchill: A Major New Reassessment of His Life in Peace and War (Oxford University Press, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Churchill's critics found much to fault. Many MPs held him responsible for the failure at the Dardanelles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1915. The labour movement long distrusted him after the Tonypandy riots of 1910 and his part in opposing the General Strike of 1926. As Chancellor he restored the gold standard in 1925 against the advice of economists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-maynard-keynes">John Maynard Keynes</a>, a move held to have caused deflation and unemployment. He supported the Black and Tans in Ireland, proposed equipping aircraft in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> with "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>" to punish "recalcitrant natives", which was never done, and as Colonial Secretary in 1921 pushed his plan for the aerial bombing of Iraq, which the Cabinet approved; the historian Yuki Tanaka sees that precedent as leading to indiscriminate bombing of Europeans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> "a seditious Middle Temple lawyer, now posing as a fakir".</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine of 1943, in which an estimated 2.1-3.8 million people died, his government was criticised for refusing more grain imports, which it ascribed to a shortage of shipping; the Viceroy, Lord Wavell, complained of "neglect, even sometimes with hostility and contempt", and the effect of British policy on the toll remains controversial. The area bombing he initiated killed about 24,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in February 1945; Max Hastings, quoted by Andrew Marr, called his later memo an attempt to distance himself from the controversy. According to Paul Addison he opposed immigration from the Commonwealth, and a 1910 letter shows support for eugenics.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers &quot;Iron Curtain&quot; speech:</p><ul><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Robert Rhodes James, Churchill: A Study in Failure 1900–1939 (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1970)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With Labour leaving the coalition, Churchill led a caretaker government into the general election of July 1945. He mishandled the campaign, claiming in a broadcast that Labour would need "some form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a>", and lost heavily to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>. As Leader of the Opposition he warned in 1946 of an "iron curtain" across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, called for a special relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and was an early advocate of European unity. Between his terms he wrote his six-volume memoir The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and in 1953 he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature.</p><p>He returned as prime minister in October 1951, nearly 77 and in poor health. His government met Macmillan's target of 300,000 new houses a year, while abroad he sought to preserve what remained of the Empire, maintaining the military response in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a> and against the Mau Mau Uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, and seeking co-operation with the United States. He was knighted in 1953, and on 23 June that year suffered a serious stroke that was kept secret. He resigned on 5 April 1955 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a>.</p><p>He declined a dukedom, stood down from the Commons in 1964 and spent his retirement at Chartwell, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and on the French Riviera. In 1963 he was made an honorary citizen of the United States. He suffered his final stroke on 10 January 1965 and died at 28 Hyde Park Gate on 24 January. After lying in state at Westminster Hall he was given a state funeral at St Paul's Cathedral and buried at Bladon.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers Retirement: 1955–1964, Death, funeral and memorials:</p><ul><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Eugene L. Rasor, Winston S. Churchill, 1874–1965: A Comprehensive Historiography and Annotated Bibliography (Greenwood Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Churchill is regarded as one of the 20th century's most significant figures and remains popular in the United Kingdom, generally seen as a victorious wartime leader who helped defend liberal democracy against fascism. Historians rank him among the greatest British prime ministers, and in a 2002 BBC poll he was voted the greatest-ever Briton. Memorials include his statue in Parliament Square, the Churchill War Rooms and Churchill College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He was the first of only eight people granted honorary citizenship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> his two changes of party bred widespread "mistrust and dislike", and some saw him as driven more by ambition than by principle; Robert Rhodes James found him "refreshingly innocent and straightforward". Biographers have called him "fundamentally conservative" and "liberal in outlook". Paul Addison describes the reformer as a "benevolent paternalist", and Jenkins credited him with "a substantial record as a social reformer". He was a staunch imperialist with a "romanticised view" of the Empire, and he has sometimes been criticised for his comments on race and for wartime decisions such as area bombing; Addison argues that he would never have tried "to stoke up racial animosity against immigrants".</p><p>He was a prolific writer of history, biography and memoir, including the four-volume A History of the English-Speaking Peoples, and an amateur painter of hundreds of canvases.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Winston Churchill&#8221; cites where it covers Death, funeral and memorials, Political ideology, Imperialism and racial views:</p><ul><li>Robert Rhodes James, Churchill: A Study in Failure 1900–1939 (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 1970)</li><li>Roy Jenkins, Churchill (Macmillan Press, 2001)</li><li>Eugene L. Rasor, Winston S. Churchill, 1874–1965: A Comprehensive Historiography and Annotated Bibliography (Greenwood Press, 2000)</li><li>Edward Adams, Liberal Epic: The Victorian Practice of History from Gibbon to Churchill (University of Virginia Press, 2011)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again<span>1844</span></a><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale<span>1871</span></a><a href="/songs/the-red-flag">The Red Flag<span>1889</span></a><a href="/songs/wide-is-my-motherland">Wide Is My Motherland<span>1936</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Winston Churchill, born at Blenheim Palace in 1874, was a soldier and war correspondent, a Liberal and Conservative minister and twice prime minister. He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> from 1940, and is honoured as a wartime leader while criticised for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, his views on race and decisions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine.</em></p>

